WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.970
If you want to find out how you can get a
pass to Halloween Horror Nights at Universal,

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:10.750
stick around to the end of this video for
details.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:15.190
One of reasons we find The Shining so disturbing
is the psychological horror and mind games,

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.210
that director Stanley Kubrick plays on us.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.820
If Universal can employ similar tactics for
it’s upcoming The Shining attraction at

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.840
Halloween Horror Nights, it’s going to be
a success.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.860
This starts with the layout of the hotel.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.910
In the movie, Kubrick subconsciously drives
the viewers crazy with impossible windows

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:35.579
and doors, hallways that shouldn’t be there,
nonsensical dead ends, and changing corridors.

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:39.140
Obviously this won’t be possible, because
it’s going to be a physical location that

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.350
we get to walk through as opposed to a movie
that takes advantage of the power of editing,

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:47.069
but they could still use the fact that this
is a temporary set to their advantage.

00:00:47.069 --> 00:00:50.640
In previous Horror Nights walkthroughs, we
see different movie locations connected by

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.280
using dark areas and fog as transitions.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.100
Instead of that, we think they could use optical
illusion matte paintings to make some hallways

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.980
look deeper than they actually are.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.579
Then we can take a turn into a room, and when
we come out, we’ll be in another location.

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.600
This will be disorienting to guests and make
it feel like you are wandering through a maze.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.469
As we wander through these winding hallways,
they can take advantage of open doors to show

00:01:11.469 --> 00:01:17.560
off iconic scenes, like where Wendy turns
to see the bear blowjob thing and a variety

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.950
of hotel guests in different rooms that stare
at us.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.740
This would be an excellent way to have the
bloody elevator scene from down a hallway

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.009
and allow blood to gush at us.

00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:32.020
It would have to be contained behind a form
of plexiglass with a drain system so it could

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.020
reset.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:37.420
There are a few rooms we don't just want to
see from a distance, like Room 237, The Gold

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.020
Room, The Colorado Lounge and the Torrance
room.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:44.159
If I don’t get to walk on that iconic carpet
pattern, then the whole thing is failure.

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:48.640
The biggest problem these locations pose is
that they are either wide open locations or

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.750
dead ends.

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:53.689
Most of the haunted houses at Universal contain
skinny corridors with one set path for guests

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:54.829
to walk on.

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:59.590
Wide open locations such as the Gold Room
and Colorado Lounge could make use of furniture

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.380
arrangements and possibly velvet rope to guide
people along a certain path.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.190
Dead end areas like the Torrence room could
make use of breakaway walls that open a new

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:10.190
path.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:13.390
For example, imagine you are being chased
by Jack Torrence through the apartment.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.080
You are led into the bathroom and Wendy shuts
the door behind you.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.690
Jack bangs on the door, says Here’s Johnny,
and then Wendy opens the window, allowing

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:24.720
guests to access a slide or ramp through the
snow to get down to safety.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.010
This scenario puts the theme park guests in
the shoes of Danny, which we think is the

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.160
most frightening way to experience the classic
scenes.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.550
The maze would have to start around the climax
of the story, where Jack begins to chase Danny

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:37.690
around the hotel.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:42.020
This allows the guests to be chased through
the entire attraction to find their escape.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:46.200
Being able to hear Jack yelling as they make
their escape can build tension while allowing

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.180
Jack to occasionally finds us during our quest.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.770
Some rooms can appear larger than us to give
off the feel that we are in the mind of a

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:57.330
child and play on having little control over
the situations we’re placed in.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:02.560
Placing the Grady Twins in our path as a blockade
would assist in keeping everyone on track.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.730
The only friendly faces would be Wendy and
Dick Hallorann as they guide us.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.020
Another major theme in the movie is mirrorism.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.670
In addition to all of the symmetrical sets
and shots in the movie, mirrors are literally

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.770
used to look into the hotel’s past.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:18.290
In fact, all of Jack's encounters with the
ghosts of the Overlook take place in front

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:21.790
of mirrors, providing a Shining reflection
into times gone by.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:25.670
I have actually theorized that Jack literally
sees these figures through mirrors.

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:29.320
Take this scene, where Jack is getting more
worked up with each mirror that he passes.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.650
Or this scene, where Jack seems to be looking
past Delbert Grady and conversing with his

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.650
reflection.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:38.090
As it turns out, the theme park industry has
found some pretty cool uses for mirrors though

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:39.840
the use of digital overlays.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.330
Remember the hitchhiking ghosts from the Haunted
Mansion?

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.560
Imagine a similar effect while you are are
traversing the depths of Room 237.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.110
You are approached by a beautiful woman, and
turn to the mirror to find she is not as she

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.110
appears.

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:54.230
The digital mirror overlays aren’t the only
technology that we can see working to the

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.360
advantage of this maze.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.340
Last year we visited a Universal walk-through
based on the Christmas horror movie, Krampus

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.690
that used temperature controlled rooms to
give theme park guests a chill, (pun intended),

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.840
when they walked into areas that are supposed
to be like an outdoor segment.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.650
This change in temperature physically heightens
our senses and puts us more on edge, making

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.730
any scares that take place in these areas
have that much greater of an effect on us.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.600
That leads us to how we want the attraction
to end.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.970
The only way to do it is to chase patrons
into the hedge maze.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:25.040
All of the walk-throughs you usually see at
Halloween Horror Nights are typically referred

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.060
to as mazes, despite the fact that there is
only one route you can take through the entire

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.720
thing in most cases.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:36.150
Having an actual maze creates a problem for
the park though, which is that people trying

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:41.000
to find their way takes time, and slows down
the already extremely long lines.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.900
Instead of having one path, we think it should
split into a few possible outcomes, each to

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.040
their own exit.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.940
This would give people a reason to go back
through to try out the different paths and

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.620
allow a unique experience every time.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:58.650
Doing this also means you would hear the screams
and terror from other guests and be unsure

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.030
if Jack was around the next turn.

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:05.560
Splitting it into a few possible exits, will
also provide relief to the slow flow of foot

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.350
traffic that keeps the lines from moving quickly.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.790
And once guests do finally make it out of
that maze, I can think of no better final

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:15.790
image to walk away from than the realistic
sculpture of Jack Nicholson frozen in the

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:17.740
snow at the end of the movie.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.430
That covers what we do want to see.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.180
We also want to cover a few cliches that we
don’t want to see.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:25.940
The Shining does have some great jump scares,
and I know they’ll do a great job of realizing

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:29.750
those in the form of a haunted house, but
I hope they don’t resort to unnecessary

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:34.430
things that don’t fit the tone of The Shining,
like people jumping out of paintings or corpses

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.310
coming to life or anything like that.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.711
I hope they don’t have the characters unnessarily
over-zombified, as we often see in these kinds

00:05:40.711 --> 00:05:41.711
of things.

00:05:41.711 --> 00:05:45.370
And finally, the psychological terror of Shining
comes from what you don’t see moving, not

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:46.560
what you do see moving.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.430
Any kind of floating chairs or dancing hedge
animals can be left out of this one.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:51.430
OK…

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:54.000
Remember at the beginning of this video how
we mentioned the details of how you can get

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.420
a pass to Halloween Horror Nights?

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.370
Well, we can’t actually provide you with
one, because we’re just some people who

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:01.780
